// @emit-error monomorphize: unsupported argument type for `v` in a call to `fnv`
// THE PRICE OF WIDENING `monoDeclAnnName`'s GATE, pinned so it cannot be silently
// un-priced. This program BUILT AND RAN before the widening and is a loud reject after —
// ten cells of the 192-cell grid move this way, all of them an inline-shape or `| null`
// parameter annotation with a CONCRETE return.
//
// IT IS AN ACCIDENTAL ACCEPT, NOT A CAPABILITY, and the NON-SHADOWING control is the whole
// argument. Delete the `type Id = string` line and rename the parameter to `T`:
//
//     function fnv<T>(v: { a: T }): boolean { return true }
//     const x: { a: string } = { a: "w" }
//
// and that program is THIS SAME DIAGNOSTIC, character for character, on master and before
// the shadow family was ever touched. An inline-shape parameter annotation on a generic
// FuncDecl is unsupported. The shadowed spelling only ever compiled because the canon pass
// (`canonEmitTypeNames`, a name rewrite with no scope) erased `Id` to `string` — the very
// type this call happens to pass — turning the signature into a concrete one that never
// reached the monomorphizer's parameter path at all. Handing it a DIFFERENT argument type
// exposed that immediately: `type Id = i32` with the same `{ a: string }` argument wrote an
// invalid module (`type mismatch: expected (ref $type), found (ref $type)`), `vl check`
// rc=0. The accept was the diagonal of a defect, not a promise the language makes.
//
// The trade was taken deliberately: 26 invalid-wasm cells become loud rejects and 15 become
// CORRECT, against these 10 — and every one of the 10 has a control that rejects with this
// message on master, so none of them is a capability the language is losing.
type Id = string

function fnv<Id>(v: { a: Id }): boolean { return true }

const x: { a: string } = { a: "w" }
const r = fnv(x)
print(r)
